  "app": {
    "title": "JALEBI — JWST Analysis of Line Emission with Bayesian Inference",
    "summary": "A person loads a spectrum, fits and subtracts its continuum, and masks lines that the model should not fit. They build a model from a list of components, setting each one's parameters with sliders against the data and residuals. Automatic detection can propose which components to include, and a grid map can locate good starting values. They then run a staged fit (grid, optimiser, MCMC) and read the parameter medians, corner plots, correlations and traces. Finally they export the parameter summary and the configuration, or run the same setup over a whole batch of targets.",
    "task": {
      "text": "Fit a physical model with several components to a spectrum all at once and get posterior estimates of every component's parameters, with their uncertainties and degeneracies.",
      "verbs": [
        "derive",
        "identify",
        "clean",
        "explore"
      ]
    },
    "data": {
      "text": "One-dimensional spectra split into instrument sub-bands, with flux and errors against wavelength, plus line lists of molecular transitions and, optionally, spectral cubes from which spectra are extracted.",
      "item": "a target's spectrum, fitted as a sum of model components",
      "shapes": [
        "time series",
        "table",
        "categories",
        "field"
      ]
    },
    "stage": {
      "text": "fitting continuum-subtracted spectra to derive physical parameters, after data reduction and before writing up",
      "terms": [
        "analyze",
        "clean"
      ]
    },
    "mechanism": {
      "text": "Workspace tabs follow the pipeline (Data, Continuum, Model, Fit, Results, Batch). In each one, a wide spectrum chart sits beside a sticky inspector of controls, and editing a control redraws the chart along with its linked residual or continuum-subtracted plot. Long fits run in the background with a progress bar and fill a results page of tables and diagnostic figures.",
      "views": [
        "line chart",
        "form",
        "table view",
        "heatmap",
        "scatter plot",
        "summary stats",
        "image viewer"
      ],
      "interactions": [
        "import",
        "inline edit",
        "select",
        "hover for detail",
        "zoom and pan",
        "toggle view",
        "exclude and refit",
        "export"
      ],
      "patterns": [
        "linked views",
        "status pipeline",
        "overview and detail"
      ]
    },
    "field": {
      "text": "LTE slab-model fitting of molecular line emission in mid-infrared spectra, in astronomy",
      "disciplines": [
        "astronomy"
      ],
      "terms": [
        "astronomy",
        "infrared spectroscopy",
        "protoplanetary disks"
      ]
    },
    "workflow": {
      "name": "fit a model with several components to a spectrum and estimate parameter posteriors",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step": "bring the data in",
          "here": "Choose a target from a scanned data root or upload a CSV or FITS file, and set its distance and radial velocity. Spectra can also be extracted from a cube by tapping the median image to place the aperture. The spectrum is drawn coloured by sub-band, with optional line-ID markers.",
          "parts": [
            "data-tab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "clean the data before analysis",
          "here": "Pick one of several continuum methods and tune its settings, or tap the plot to place spline anchors. Mask H I, H₂ and OH prompt lines or any extra ranges. The continuum-subtracted spectrum and the noise for each sub-band update live.",
          "parts": [
            "continuum-tab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "identify which components are present",
          "here": "\"Detect molecules\" fits templates of every candidate at once and keeps those that pass a ΔBIC threshold. They can be applied as the component list or added as missing components only.",
          "parts": [
            "detect-molecules"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "build the model and compare it with the data",
          "here": "Add component cards by molecule and line list. Set log N, T, log R, velocity and width with sliders, and tie, group or fix parameters. The model overlay, the residual in σ and χ² redraw. \"Auto areas (NNLS)\" solves the emitting areas, and a (log N, T) Δχ² grid map pushes its best point to the sliders.",
          "parts": [
            "component-cards",
            "model-tab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "compute a value from the data",
          "here": "Choose stages (grid, optimise, MCMC) and settings for the sampler and optimiser, then press \"Run fit\" and follow the progress bar. \"Quick errors (Laplace)\" gives approximate uncertainties at the optimum.",
          "parts": [
            "fit-tab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "check the result and its uncertainties",
          "here": "A parameter table shows medians and 16/84 % intervals. Below it are the best fit across the full spectrum and in the fit windows, the posterior predictive, a corner plot for the chosen components, the correlation matrix and the walker traces.",
          "parts": [
            "results-tab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "export the result",
          "here": "Download the summary CSV of parameters and export the configuration as YAML, or reuse that configuration in the Batch tab to fit every scanned target.",
          "parts": [
            "results-tab",
            "sidebar-config",
            "batch-tab"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distinctive": [
        "All components are fitted to one spectrum at once, so overlapping bands compete for the same flux. Opacity groups and isotopologue ties link the parameters of different components.",
        "Emitting areas are linear parameters solved exactly by non-negative least squares at every step, so only column density and temperature are searched.",
        "The fit runs in stages (a grid, then an optimiser, then MCMC), and the results show the degeneracies between parameters and between components, not only error bars.",
        "Continuum placement is treated as a major source of systematic error, and the continuum and masks stay editable, with the result shown live.",
        "Instrument sub-bands are kept separate throughout, with each sub-band's noise estimated and coloured separately."
      ]
    }
  },
